{"black_box_warning":{"current_value":"WARNING: RISKS FROM CONCOMITANT USE WITH OPIOIDS; ABUSE, MISUSE, AND ADDICTION; and DEPENDENCE AND WITHDRAWAL REACTIONS Concomitant use of benzodiazepines and opioids may result in profound sedation, respiratory depression, coma, and death. Reserve concomitant prescribing of these drugs in patients for whom alternative treatment options are inadequate. Limit dosages and durations to the minimum required. Follow patients for signs and symptoms of respiratory depression and sedation (See WARNINGS and PRECAUTIONS ). The use of benzodiazepines, including Clorazepate dipotassium tablets, exposes users to risks of abuse, misuse, and addiction, which can lead to overdose or death. Abuse and misuse of benzodiazepines commonly involve concomitant use of other medications, alcohol, and/or illicit substances, which is associated with an increased frequency of serious adverse outcomes. Before prescribing Clorazepate dipotassium tablets and throughout out treatment, assess each patient’s risk for abuse, misuse, and addiction (See WARNINGS ). The continued use of benzodiazepines, including Clorazepate dipotassium tablets, may lead to clinically significant physical dependence. The risks of dependence and withdrawal increase with longer treatment duration and higher daily dose. Abrupt discontinuation or rapid dosage reduction of Clorazepate dipotassium tablets after continued use may precipitate acute withdrawal reactions, which can be life- threatening. To reduce the risk of withdrawal reactions, use a gradual taper to discontinue Clorazepate dipotassium tablets or reduce the dosage (See DOSAGE AND ADMINISTRATION and WARNINGS ).","fda_source_urls":["https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id%3A%22919cbd07-f587-4005-acff-26213dd1d1fb%22&limit=5","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/017105s085lbl.pdf"],"proposed_value":"No change to warning text; replace the source metadata with an FDA/openFDA or Drugs@FDA/accessdata source instead of DailyMed.","rationale":"The boxed warning content is consistent with FDA labeling for clorazepate/Tranxene and the row’s FDA/openFDA set-id metadata. The current source field cites DailyMed, which is not permissible for FDA black-box verification under the audit rule.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified"},"blocking_questions":[],"fact_checks":[{"current_value":"clorazepate dipotassium","field":"generic_name","proposed_value":"clorazepate dipotassium","rationale":"FDA labeling, Orange Book, and NCBI MeSH identify the substance as clorazepate dipotassium.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/017105s085lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/68003009"]},{"current_value":"clorazepate","field":"alternate_generic_names","proposed_value":"clorazepate; dipotassium chlorazepate; chlorazepate; clorazepic acid","rationale":"Current alias is valid, but NCBI MeSH also lists dipotassium chlorazepate, chlorazepate, and clorazepic acid as entry terms that should not become separate rows.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/68003009"]},{"current_value":"Clorazepate Dipotassium; Gen-Xene; Tranxene; Tranxene SD","field":"trade_names","proposed_value":"Clorazepate Dipotassium; Gen-Xene; Tranxene; Tranxene SD","rationale":"Tranxene is listed in NCBI MeSH and FDA/Drugs@FDA labeling. Tranxene SD and Gen-Xene are supported as clorazepate dipotassium aliases/products in NCBI PubChem/FDA-linked drug information and historical FDA Orange Book snippets.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/68003009","https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/167305","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/nda/2002/017105_S062_TRANXENE_AP.pdf","https://www.fda.gov/media/71474/download"]},{"current_value":"Yes","field":"available_in_us","proposed_value":"Yes","rationale":"FDA Orange Book materials list clorazepate dipotassium oral tablet products, and FDA/openFDA has current clorazepate dipotassium labeling.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/media/71474/download","https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-approvals-and-databases/orange-book-data-files","https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id%3A%22919cbd07-f587-4005-acff-26213dd1d1fb%22&limit=5"]},{"current_value":"Focal","field":"epilepsy_type","proposed_value":"Focal","rationale":"FDA labeling states clorazepate/Tranxene tablets are indicated as adjunctive therapy for partial seizures; focal is the modern category corresponding to partial seizures.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/017105s085lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Oral tablet currently; oral capsule historical/discontinued in Orange Book","field":"formulations_available","proposed_value":"Oral tablet currently; oral capsule historical/discontinued in Orange Book","rationale":"FDA Orange Book materials support current oral tablet listings and historical/non-marketed capsule products for clorazepate dipotassium/Tranxene.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/media/71474/download","https://www.fda.gov/media/72973/download","https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-approvals-and-databases/orange-book-data-files"]},{"current_value":"Approved Prior to Jan 1, 1982 (FDA Orange Book)","field":"year_fda_cleared","proposed_value":"Approved prior to Jan 1, 1982 (FDA Orange Book; NDA 017105).","rationale":"The Orange Book is the appropriate source for this field; the deterministic check compared the value to a selected label, which is not expected to contain the Orange Book approval-date phrase.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/media/71474/download","https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-approvals-and-databases/orange-book-data-files"]},{"current_value":"FDA Orange Book pre-1982 benzodiazepine ASM; FDA-labeled as adjunctive therapy in partial seizures and also for anxiety and acute alcohol withdrawal.","field":"status_or_notes","proposed_value":"FDA Orange Book pre-1982 benzodiazepine ASM; FDA-labeled as adjunctive therapy in partial seizures and also for anxiety and acute alcohol withdrawal.","rationale":"FDA labeling supports partial-seizure adjunctive therapy, anxiety, and acute alcohol withdrawal indications; Orange Book supports the pre-1982 approval status.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/017105s085lbl.pdf","https://www.fda.gov/media/71474/download"]},{"current_value":"N/A 0%: FDA label lists drowsiness, dizziness, GI complaints, nervousness, blurred vision, dry mouth, headache, mental confusion, insomnia, skin rashes, fatigue, ataxia, irritability, diplopia, depression, tremor, and slurred speech without quantified incidence percentages.","field":"adverse_symptoms_percentages","proposed_value":"N/A: FDA label lists drowsiness, dizziness, GI complaints, nervousness, blurred vision, dry mouth, headache, mental confusion, insomnia, skin rashes, fatigue, ataxia, irritability, diplopia, depression, tremor, and slurred speech without quantified incidence percentages.","rationale":"The FDA label lists these adverse reactions qualitatively. It does not support an incidence of 0%; removing the 0% notation avoids implying no adverse-event incidence.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/017105s085lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Nordiazepam active metabolite about 40-50 h; FDA label also describes serum half-life as about 2 days.","field":"half_life_range","proposed_value":"Nordiazepam active metabolite about 40-50 h; FDA label also describes serum half-life as about 2 days.","rationale":"FDA labeling states serum half-life is about 2 days and that nordiazepam has an apparent plasma half-life of about 40 to 50 hours.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/017105s085lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Liver metabolism; excreted primarily in urine per FDA label.","field":"major_organ_for_metabolism","proposed_value":"Liver metabolism; excreted primarily in urine per FDA label.","rationale":"FDA labeling states the drug is metabolized in the liver and excreted primarily in urine, with urinary metabolites described.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/017105s085lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"90 mg/day maximum recommended daily dose in selected FDA label.","field":"maximum_approved_daily_dose","proposed_value":"90 mg/day maximum recommended daily dose in selected FDA label.","rationale":"FDA labeling states the maximum recommended total daily dose is 90 mg and that adjunctive antiepileptic dosing in patients over 12 years should not exceed 90 mg/day.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/017105s085lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"22.5 mg/day initial adjunctive partial-seizure dose for patients older than 12 years in selected FDA label.","field":"minimum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"22.5 mg/day initial adjunctive partial-seizure dose for patients older than 12 years in selected FDA label.","rationale":"FDA labeling gives an initial adjunctive antiepileptic dose of 7.5 mg three times daily for patients over 12 years, equal to 22.5 mg/day.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/017105s085lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Adjunctive partial seizures: FDA label starts patients older than 12 years at 7.5 mg three times daily with gradual adjustment.","field":"typical_doses_per_day","proposed_value":"Adjunctive partial seizures: FDA label starts patients older than 12 years at 7.5 mg three times daily with gradual adjustment.","rationale":"This matches FDA dosage labeling for adjunctive antiepileptic use in patients over 12 years.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/017105s085lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Benzodiazepine prodrug to nordiazepam; FDA labeling describes benzodiazepine CNS depressant pharmacology and notes benzodiazepines interact at GABA-A sites.","field":"mechanism_of_action","proposed_value":"Benzodiazepine prodrug to nordiazepam; FDA labeling describes benzodiazepine CNS depressant pharmacology and notes benzodiazepines interact at GABA-A sites.","rationale":"FDA labeling states clorazepate has benzodiazepine CNS-depressant pharmacology, is rapidly decarboxylated to nordiazepam, and notes benzodiazepines interact at GABA-A sites. NCBI MeSH lists GABA modulator pharmacologic action.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/017105s085lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/68003009"]},{"current_value":"Moderate","field":"mechanism_confidence","proposed_value":"Moderate","rationale":"The source-backed mechanism is class-based benzodiazepine/GABA-A modulation plus nordiazepam formation; a moderate confidence rating is reasonable as an internal evidence tier.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/017105s085lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/68003009"]},{"current_value":"N/A - selected FDA label does not define clinically meaningful enzyme induction or inhibition.","field":"enzyme_inducing_or_inhibiting","proposed_value":"N/A - selected FDA label does not define clinically meaningful enzyme induction or inhibition.","rationale":"FDA labeling describes CNS depressant/opioid interaction risks but does not define clorazepate as a clinically meaningful enzyme inducer or inhibitor.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/017105s085lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"No QT interval effect described in selected FDA label.","field":"qt_interval_effect","proposed_value":"No QT interval effect described in selected FDA label.","rationale":"The selected FDA labeling reviewed does not describe QT interval effects. This supports the source-bounded statement, not a broader proof of no QT effect.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/017105s085lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"No known meaningful QT effect","field":"filter_qt_effect","proposed_value":"Unknown/limited","rationale":"The detailed row text only establishes that QT interval effects are not described in the selected FDA label; it does not directly establish a clinically verified 'no known meaningful QT effect' category.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"insufficient_evidence","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2023/017105s085lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"clorazepate; Tranxene; Tranxene SD; Gen-Xene","field":"pubmed_search_aliases","proposed_value":"clorazepate; clorazepate dipotassium; dipotassium chlorazepate; chlorazepate; Tranxene; Tranxene SD; Gen-Xene; Tranxilium","rationale":"Current aliases are valid, but NCBI MeSH/PubChem support additional important search aliases and spellings that should prevent duplicate rows and improve PubMed recall.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/6800